<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>CITY ATTORNEY’S FINANCIAL ANALYSIS OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE Q</strong></p>
<p><strong>FINANCIAL ANALYSIS OF THE CITY OF RICHMOND GENERAL OBLIGATION BOND MEASURE FOR FIRE STATION IMPROVEMENTS (NOVEMBER 3, 2026)</strong></p>
<p><strong>Finance Director’s statement on the fiscal impact of this Measure:</strong></p>
<p>If approved by voters, this Measure would authorize the City of Richmond to issue up to $120 million in general obligation bonds to finance capital improvements to fire stations and related public safety facilities. Projects eligible for funding include the repair, reconstruction, modernization, and seismic improvement of aging and deteriorating fire stations; renovation of firefighter living quarters; replacement of electrical, roofing, and plumbing systems; and other upgrades necessary to support rapid 911 emergency medical and fire response. Bond proceeds may be used only for voter-approved capital improvements and related eligible costs.</p>
<p>Debt service on the bonds would be repaid exclusively from a voter-approved ad valorem property tax, not from the City’s General Fund. Based on current assessed valuation projections, the City estimates the following tax rates associated with repayment:</p>
<p>• Estimated average annual tax rate:<br />
$22.15 per $100,000 of assessed valuation.</p>
<p>• Estimated highest annual tax rate:<br />
$24.06 per $100,000 of assessed valuation, anticipated beginning in fiscal year 2028.</p>
<p>• Estimated total debt service (principal + interest):<br />
$249,749,206 over the life of the bonds.</p>
<p>Actual tax rates may vary depending on changes in assessed valuation, interest rates at the time of each bond sale, and the timing and amount of bonds issued.</p>
<p>Bond proceeds will be deposited in a separate account dedicated to the authorized projects. Expenditures will be subject to annual independent audits, annual reporting to the City Council, and oversight by a community oversight committee. Completion of all identified projects is not guaranteed if total project costs exceed available bond proceeds.</p>
<p>This fiscal analysis is prepared in accordance with Richmond Municipal Code Section 2.16.070 and applicable provisions of the California Elections Code. It is informational only and does not advocate for or against the Measure.</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE Q</strong></p>
<p>Richmond’s emergency response infrastructure is failing us—and our first responders.</p>
<p>When a heart attack, stroke, serious injury, or other emergency strikes, <strong>every second counts</strong>. But our aging, undersized stations can’t accommodate enough firefighters or the latest equipment needed to respond quickly. Slower response times put residents at risk when speed is a matter of life or death.</p>
<p>Today, some Richmond firefighters work in seismically unsafe buildings, with cracking foundations, leaky roofs, mold, and outdated electrical systems, jeopardizing their health and safety while they work to protect ours.</p>
<p>Measure Q is a responsible, accountable plan to fix Richmond’s emergency response infrastructure. It authorizes $120 million in bonds to modernize and rehabilitate unsafe, deteriorating firefighter facilities, which will help improve emergency response times and protect the health and safety of our firefighters.</p>
<p>Key investments will include:</p>
<p>• <strong>Improving</strong> facilities and infrastructure to support rapid 911 emergency medical response, matching the standard of care available across the County.</p>
<p>• <strong>Bringing</strong> firefighter apparatus, equipment, and facilities up to current safety and industry standards.</p>
<p>• <strong>Rehabilitating</strong> unsafe firefighter housing, including removing mold and asbestos.</p>
<p>• <strong>Replacing</strong> deteriorating electrical systems, leaky pipes, roofs, and other aging infrastructure in fire stations.</p>
<p>• <strong>Bringing</strong> aging fire stations, some more than 80 years old, up to current building and safety codes.</p>
<p>• <strong>Improving</strong> the resiliency of fire stations and emergency response facilities during earthquakes, wildfires, and other disasters.</p>
<p>Measure Q requires strict accountability: independent financial audits and mandatory citizen oversight ensure every dollar is spent only on approved projects.</p>
<p>Richmond families deserve fast emergency response times and firefighters who are safe, equipped, and ready when they are needed most. Vote Yes on Measure Q to protect our neighbors, our first responders, and our community’s future.</p>
<p>Join Richmond firefighters, emergency medical responders, and neighbors across every district. Vote Yes on Measure Q.</p>
<p>United Business Organization, Juan Muñoz, President<br />
St. Cornelius Catholic Church, Rev. Sergio Mora, Pastor<br />
Myrtle L. Braxton, Senior Citizen<br />
Karla M. Palma Melgar, Registered Nurse<br />
Fernando Ramirez, Richmond Firefighter</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE Q</strong></p>
<p>Measure Q promises strict accountability, but Richmond’s own records show that the City bent the rules to put it on the ballot.</p>
<p>Before asking voters to approve $120 million in new debt, city staff bypassed their own procurement rules.</p>
<p>Records show SCI Consulting Group performed roughly $97,000 of “Phase 1” polling and feasibility work, including a taxpayer-funded poll fielded May 12-29, 2026, <strong>before</strong> the City Council approved the <strong>sole-source</strong> contract covering that work.</p>
<p>The sole-source justification was dated April 16, 2026, yet wasn’t brought to Council for approval until the June 16 consent calendar, with a contract start date <strong>backdated</strong> to April 28. A second phase adds $150,000 for “informational and ballot-readiness services,” bringing the contract to $247,000 without fixing anything at a fire station.</p>
<p>The City has known for years that its fire stations need repair, yet rather than opening the consulting work to competitive bid through a standard RFQ/RFP process, a step that could have saved taxpayers money, staff chose a single, sole-source vendor without prior Council approval. If they can’t follow their own rules asking for money, why trust them spending it?</p>
<p>While Measure Q authorizes $120 million in bonds, it puts taxpayers on the hook for <strong>repayments estimated at $249,749,206</strong>. That falls on top of the school bonds and parcel taxes Richmond property owners already pay, including $52 a year for a hospital that closed in 2015.</p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure Q.</strong></p>
<p>Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President<br />
Denise Gianni, Richmond Resident</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE Q</strong></p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure Q.</strong></p>
<p>Richmond’s firefighters deserve safe, functional stations. Measure Q is the wrong way to pay for them.</p>
<p>Richmond property owners are already among the most heavily taxed in California. On top of the basic one percent rate, they pay roughly 0.42 percent more every year in voter-approved bond levies, over $420 per $100,000 of assessed value. West Contra Costa school bonds alone cost about $250 per $100,000, the highest school-bond rate in the county. Community college bonds, two BART bonds, and a regional park bond pile on further. Homeowners still pay a $52 hospital parcel tax for Doctors Medical Center, which closed in 2016 and was demolished. Enough is enough.</p>
<p>Measure Q involves borrowing $120 million and forces property owners to repay $249,749,206, more than double, with the tax collected all the way through 2060. You would pay for decades, at interest, to fix leaky roofs, old pipes, mold and electrical systems that ordinary budgeting should have maintained.</p>
<p>And Richmond does not need to borrow. The city just received the Chevron settlement, roughly $550 million over ten years. The entire fire-facility need it has identified is about $115 million. Two years of settlement money covers all of it, with no new tax and no interest.</p>
<p>The city knows this. When Councilmember Bana pressed for a backup plan, City Manager Shasa Curl confirmed that if voters say no, Richmond will simply use settlement funds to upgrade the stations. A no vote puts no firehouse at risk. Richmond’s own poll agrees: 81 percent said the city “should be more responsible and spend the money it already has.”</p>
<p>Fix the stations with the money already in hand. Do not pile another quarter-billion-dollar tax on property owners who already pay enough.</p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure Q.</strong></p>
<p>Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President<br />
Denise Gianni, Richmond Resident</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE Q</strong></p>
<p>When an emergency strikes, Richmond families rely on our firefighters to respond quickly and save lives.<br />
Measure Q makes critical investments in the aging facilities and equipment they depend on to protect us each<br />
and every day. The argument against Measure Q ignores these urgent needs and misrepresents the facts.<br />
Here are the facts:<br />
Fact: Some Richmond firefighters work in seismically unsafe fire stations with cracking foundations, leaky<br />
roofs, mold, asbestos, and outdated electrical systems. Measure Q will repair and modernize these deteriorating facilities to protect firefighter health and safety.<br />
Fact: Rapid 911 emergency medical and fire response depends on reliable facilities and infrastructure. Measure Q makes critical improvements to help first responders respond when every second counts.<br />
Fact: Firefighters need modern, reliable equipment to do their jobs safely and effectively. Measure Q will bring firefighter apparatus, equipment, and facilities up to current safety and industry standards.<br />
Fact: Strict accountability protects taxpayers. Independent financial audits and mandatory citizen oversight ensure Measure Q funds are spent only on approved projects.<br />
Fact: Some Richmond fire stations are more than 80 years old. Improving aging fire stations and emergency<br />
response facilities will help ensure they remain operational during earthquakes, wildfires, and other major disasters.<br />
Richmond families deserve fast emergency response times and firefighters who are safe, equipped, and ready<br />
when they are needed most.<br />
Join Richmond firefighters, emergency medical responders, and neighbors across every district.<br />
Vote Yes on Measure Q.</p>
<p>Sergio Rios, Small Business Owner, Bob’s Cleaners<br />
Zachary Jenssen, Firefighter<br />
Edgar O. Hall, Senior Citizen<br />
Barbara Whittom, Church-Clerk, The Bible<br />
Andrew Wilke, Richmond High Music Director</p>
