<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>FULL TEXT OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE Q</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>MEASURE TO BE SUBMITTED TO THE VOTERS AUTHORIZING</strong><br />
<strong>THE CITY OF RICHMOND, CALIFORNIA TO</strong><br />
<strong>ISSUE GENERAL OBLIGATION BONDS</strong><br />
<strong>TO FINANCE MUNICIPAL IMPROVEMENT PROJECTS</strong></p>
<p><strong>Section 1. Findings.</strong></p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, rapid response to fires, medical emergencies, and other disasters depends on a fire department infrastructure that is safe, reliable, and operational when needed, and much of the fire station infrastructure of the City of Richmond, California (the “City”) has aged well beyond its intended useful life and no longer reliably meets that need; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, deferred maintenance and the physical deterioration of the City’s aging fire stations, if left unaddressed, will continue to increase over time, resulting in higher repair costs, greater risk of facility failure, and increased risk to firefighters, paramedics, and the public they serve; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, the City, like many California communities, faces heightened exposure to earthquakes, wildfires, and other natural disasters, and it is essential that public safety infrastructure be capable of remaining operational and supporting emergency response efforts during and after such events; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, modern fire protection and emergency medical services require facilities and working conditions that meet current health, safety, and operational standards, and the City has an ongoing responsibility to maintain its public safety infrastructure accordingly; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, it is in the best interest of the City and necessary for the preservation of the public peace, health, and safety to issue general obligation bonds to finance the acquisition, construction, and improvement of fire stations and related facilities, as more particularly described in this Measure, in order to protect the public and ensure the continued delivery of essential fire and emergency medical services; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, the City intends to issue general obligation bonds to finance the acquisition, construction and improvement of municipal improvement projects, and the City Council has found and determined that the public interest and necessity demand the issuance of general obligation bonds to finance the cost of acquisition, construction and improvement of certain municipal improvements, consisting generally of: the repair, reconstruction, and seismic retrofit of aging and seismically unsafe fire stations; the renovation of firefighter living quarters, including abatement and removal of mold and asbestos; the repair and replacement of deteriorating electrical, roofing, and plumbing systems; and other improvements to fire stations and related facilities necessary to support rapid 911 emergency medical and fire response, all as more particularly described in this Measure; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, existing funds and funding sources are inadequate to finance said improvements; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, the issuance of general obligation bonds is the most cost-effective means available to the City to finance said improvements, as general obligation bonds typically bear lower interest rates than other forms of financing due to the security provided by the City’s ad valorem tax pledge, thereby minimizing the overall cost to taxpayers of funding these necessary public safety improvements;</p>
<p><strong>Section 2. Object and Purpose of Bonds.</strong></p>
<p>This Measure authorizes the issuance of general obligation bonds (the “Bonds”), the object and purpose of which is to finance the cost of the acquisition, construction and improvement of certain property to be used for municipal purposes (the “Improvements”), consisting of the following:</p>
<p>(a) Repair, replace, or reconstruct fire stations that are deteriorating and/or seismically unsafe, including stations that are approximately 80 years old, with reconstruction undertaken where the City Council determines that replacement is more practical than renovation;</p>
<p>(b) Renovate, upgrade, reconstruct, or acquire firefighter living quarters, whether located at a fire station or elsewhere, including abatement and removal of mold and asbestos, to meet current (then-applicable, i.e., as of the time of construction) building code and industry standards;</p>
<p>(c) Repair or replace deteriorating electrical systems and infrastructure at fire stations;</p>
<p>(d) Repair or replace deteriorating roofs, plumbing, and piping at fire stations to address leaks and deferred maintenance;</p>
<p>(e) Make structural, seismic, and other resiliency improvements at fire stations, in addition to those described above, to improve their ability to remain operational during and after earthquakes, fires, floods, and other disasters or emergencies;</p>
<p>(f) Repair, replace, or install fixtures and other permanently installed building systems at fire stations, including emergency generators, HVAC systems, and communications infrastructure, and make other building repairs and improvements at fire stations, to address conditions that impair their function in supporting rapid 911 emergency medical and fire response; and</p>
<p>(g) Construct, renovate, upgrade, reconstruct, or acquire training facilities, administrative offices, apparatus bays, and other facilities supporting Fire Department operations.</p>
<p>The Improvements described above together constitute, and are intended by the City Council to constitute, a single, unified plan and program of municipal improvements for the City, and the listing of individual projects is intended to describe the components of that unified plan.</p>
<p>The authorized Improvements also include all work, facilities and expenditures necessary and incidental to the projects described above. Examples of such work, facilities, and expenditures include, but are not limited to: costs of design, engineering, architect and other professional services, inspections, site preparation, utilities, landscaping, construction management and other planning and permitting, legal, accounting and similar costs; a customary construction contingency; demolition and disposal of existing structures; rental or construction of storage facilities and other space on an interim basis for materials and other equipment and furnishings displaced during construction; addressing unforeseen conditions revealed by construction or renovation, and other necessary improvements required to comply with existing building codes and other applicable law, including the Americans with Disabilities Act; costs of the bond election; bond issuance costs; project administration during the duration of such projects; and financing and interest costs on the Bonds. Proceeds of the Bonds shall be applied only to the acquisition or improvement of real property, including fixtures and other equipment constituting improvements to real property, and to costs necessary or incidental thereto, consistent with Section 1(b)(2) of Article XIII A of the California Constitution.</p>
<p>The final costs, locations, designs, layouts and other components of the Improvements will be determined as plans are finalized, construction bids are awarded, and projects are completed. The City Council shall determine the priority, order, and timing of the Improvements, the Improvements may be undertaken and completed in any order, and the completion of any individual project is not contingent upon the completion of any other project. The City Council cannot guarantee that the Bonds will provide sufficient funds to allow completion of all needed Improvements.</p>
<p><strong>Section 3. Estimated Cost of Improvements.</strong></p>
<p>The estimated cost of the Improvements is $120,000,000. The estimated cost includes legal or other fees, the costs of printing the Bonds, and other costs and expenses incidental to or connected with the authorization, issuance and sale of the Bonds. To the extent the Improvements financed are revenue-producing public works, the cost of the Improvements may also include bond interest estimated to accrue during the construction period and for a period of not to exceed 12 months after completion of construction.</p>
<p><strong>Section 4. Principal Amount of Bonds.</strong></p>
<p>The aggregate principal amount of the Bonds to be issued is not to exceed $120,000,000.</p>
<p><strong>Section 5. Maximum Interest Rate.</strong></p>
<p>The maximum rate of interest to be paid on the Bonds shall be 12% per annum.</p>
<p><strong>Section 6. Accountability Requirements.</strong></p>
<p>The following accountability measures apply to the issuance of the Bonds pursuant to this Measure:</p>
<p>(a) The specific purpose of the Bonds is to finance the Improvements; and</p>
<p>(b) The proceeds from the sale of the Bonds will be used only for the purposes specified in this Measure and not for any other purpose; and</p>
<p>(c) The proceeds of the Bonds will be deposited into a separate account to be created and held by the City; and</p>
<p>(d) The Director of Finance of the City shall file a report with the Richmond City Council at least annually showing the amount of Bond proceeds collected and expended, and the stat us of the Improvements; and</p>
<p>(e) The City Council shall appoint a committee comprised of residents of the City to provide community oversight over the expenditure of the Bond proceeds. The size, composition, and specific responsibilities of the Community Oversight Committee will be determined by separate action of the City Council prior to the issuance of any of the Bonds; and</p>
<p>(f) All expenditures also will be subject to an annual independent financial audit to confirm that Bond expenditures are consistent with the intent of the Measure.</p>
